{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law","requirement":"A landlord may not hold a security deposit above one and one-half months' rent or raise it more than 10% a year, and must hold it in trust and return it with interest, less itemized deductions, within 30 days of the tenancy ending.","key_value":"1.5 months' rent; annual deposit increase max 10%","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in New Jersey; no building-age or unit-count limit stated. Seasonal rentals of 125 consecutive days or fewer are exempt from the deposit-holding and interest rules.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All New Jersey residential tenancies; seasonal rentals of 125 consecutive days or fewer exempt from deposit-holding and interest rules."},"exemptions":"A landlord need not collect a deposit at all. Deposits for seasonal use or rental (a term of not more than 125 consecutive days for a person with a permanent residence elsewhere) are exempt from the deposit-holding and interest requirements.","penalty":"Tenant may sue for double the amount of the security deposit the tenant contends was wrongfully withheld, plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees if successful.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-19 et seq.","quoted_span":"The maximum-security deposit to be collected by the landlord cannot be more than one and one-half times one month’s rent ( N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.2 ). It can be less. Any additional yearly security deposit increase may not exceed 10% of the current security deposit.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-01","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Safe Housing Act security deposit return after domestic violence lease termination","requirement":"When a tenant ends a lease under the Safe Housing Act for domestic violence, the landlord must make the deposit and interest available within 15 business days and notify the tenant in writing within 3 business days where to collect it.","key_value":"15 business days to make the deposit available; 3 business days' notice","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who terminate a lease under the Safe Housing Act because of domestic violence; the law does not apply to transient or seasonal rentals.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Tenants terminating a lease under the Safe Housing Act due to domestic violence."},"exemptions":"The law does not apply to transient or seasonal rentals.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6","quoted_span":"If a tenant terminates the lease agreement prior to the expiration of the lease pursuant to the Safe Housing Act , (N.J.S.A. 46:8-9.6), the tenant is entitled to the return of their security deposit. Within 15 business days after the lease is terminated, the landlord shall make available the return of the tenant’s security deposit, plus any interest earned, to the tenant or the tenant’s agent.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
